  "name": "Introduction",
  "problem": "A **polyhedron** is a $3$-dimensional shape where each side is a polygon. These flat polygonal sides are called **faces**.\n\n**Prisms** are polyhedrons made of two identical polygonal faces (called **bases**) connected by equal parallel line segments. If these line segments are perpendicular to the bases, we have a **right** prism.\n\nLet's take a look at some common prisms.\n\n- A **triangular (right) prism** consists of two identical triangular faces connected by equal parallel line segments.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14230697/assets/9563bd4f9c40ff5921998ed5840f8e5b43fb1af579a64a06a14782be4a86ece2.png)\n\n- A **rectangular (right) prism** consists of two identical rectangular faces connected by equal parallel line segments. Notice that each face is a rectangle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14230697/assets/39ea3dd56722c7fb05ba275a054ff46479e3ee96851dafdd66fe09455a517f1a.png)\n\n- A **cube** is a special case of a rectangular prism that consists of six equal square faces.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14230697/assets/415d03a9a886793eee4b4d16e50af26002c0881279481b2e70773b8b7bff02a5.png)\n\nBy adding more sides to the polygonal base, we can make more prisms. For example, the following shape is a pentagonal prism.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14230697/assets/8f4dd8fe8c120eabbe5a1ad6413f6f0cd9cd3d22efe8b13e25ac3d2a916f3258.png)\n\n \n\nWe can also construct hexagonal prisms, heptagonal prisms, etc.",
